</p>=0D=0A                          </mj-raw>=0D=0A                        =
  <h3>Early Learning</h3>=0A<p>I have a feeling that the stuff you learn <e=
m>riiiiight</em> as you are getting into all this making websites stuff sti=
cks with you harder than what you learned, say, yesterday. That stuff, what=
ever it is, feels like foundational knowledge that ingrains itself. Other t=
hings you learn are in <em>relation</em> to that foundation.</p>=0A<p>As su=
ch, it matters what educational material starter-outers are exposed to! Tha=
t=E2=80=99s why I=E2=80=99m a fan of early courses from long-timers like <a=
 href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/KtmnpFtmbjDa6xs5eaE9lg~~/AABEfhA=
~/DbcIBBaph-0offnpR9AE9K9zlGMTO4AHim-nepNSdEpTGbL9MJm_hn5LjgeIMjQiAT3yAU1E8=
n__FqAXt6RaG5Yu79WVQRsHbhhSO5VfPi8NKh52dzinImt_iNYzaCxy">Geoff</a> and <a h=
ref=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/jUQJ_9ErUCqpg6UasxW6wQ~~/AABEfhA~/=
41NM0x9KV9SM_wLCtPzb_JqTtYdaZkNWRUl9Ad3MOkOmxelaZfBtDHWQcQi7id0wdYQnsnZZ-OC=
A5F35HpHq5O8LB0ll-9O5bKK9MrO4pq9BcgpflMkxGba1XQcp__qN">Blake</a> or the fre=
e <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/z6_tQw2o-gT4u56q2PQGMw~~/AAB=
EfhA~/PPafJny9aCLOoYUfTkZPaXGm9k4Tdp2RAT0EwArKhuG3TI578UAtysYVMVrc_fV2nPDLi=
Es-n1-aiBpUDHrciuEiN927vrL937yA0FhPUkIjNy60P3Grv0mkUMoSFgNSiyEQ75EGwjyAqlIy=
lYUllQ~~">Frontend Masters Bootcamp</a>. I=E2=80=99m also encouraged when a=
ctual university courses run with really solid foundational knowledge. For =
example, Cyd <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/pKCGF1yk7GELy-QFi=
PiWvw~~/AABEfhA~/6eAyq4ALSyS3pXeF6QjxMV_gD-nxQ1fsOVr9LyMd7sZsu6NbX5rxPEaZ20=
dD5XPTilWT5_L-FDnV7cpJUT3M3EzNUEWQTXc7vC-WipcbCk2n3kz1AyS_p-4J8Hci1HyUq8nE6=
tG_vH9xJYsZDUiUQ-sddFtBW1XDHo2combgeAAiEbleMp-yXPCinf3091TA">says they teac=
h progressive enhancement to their students</a> getting an Associate Degree=
 in Frontend Design and Development at the University of Applied Sciences i=
n Amsterdam.</p>=0A<blockquote><p>We teach our students to build features i=
n three steps:</p>=0A <ol>=0A<li> First determine the core user task, and i=
mplement it in the most robust way possible in HTML</li>=0A=09=09<li> Layer=
 on additional capabilities with CSS</li>=0A=09=09<li> And finally (optiona=
lly) add JavaScript when the browser allows (or needs) it</li>=0A=09</ol>=
=0A=09</blockquote>=0A<p>A classic:</p>=0A<blockquote><p>Filtering, for exa=
mple, is usually treated as a client-side interaction problem. But at its c=
ore, filtering is really about narrowing down information. And HTML already=
 gives us a way to do that natively: a form that submits a request and retu=
rns a refined result.</p></blockquote>=0A<p>I tend to agree that filtering =
is a good concern to make server-side first. And that is what this is sayin=
g. HTML can=E2=80=99t do filtering alone; it can just submit a form to a UR=
L (effectively reloading the page with query params) that can ask a back-en=
d language to return filtered results. In a way, this approach is saying: <=
em>it=E2=80=99s also possible the HTML doesn=E2=80=99t even contain all the=
 data you want to filter. The server has better access to that data, so let=
 it return the filtered data</em>.</p>=0A<p>I hadn=E2=80=99t really thought=
 of it this clearly before, but leaning into progressive enhancement, while=
 it feels like an approach taught to front-end developers, is really a tech=
nique that leans heavily on back-end developers. Or a full-stack concern, a=
s it were.</p>=0A<p>I also kinda get why some groan at the idea. Like if yo=
u=E2=80=99ve got a <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> of the 10 movies Paul Thomas =
Anderson directed, adding filtering and sorting to that table is a handful =
of lines of JavaScript (perhaps), which feels much less daunting than desig=
ning an API of sorts to handle the work on the backend, and having it refre=
sh the entire page to do the work, which feels a little antiquated. But if =
you do it the right way and give yourself that API, now you=E2=80=99ve got =
a re-usable system for displaying, say, the 5,241 films ever nominated for =
an Oscar as well. Websites tend to grow and change in scope, which is anoth=
er lesson to learn. So is premature optimization, but I digress.</p>=0A<p>I=
=E2=80=99ll let Kilian do that instead, with <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpos=
tmail2.com/f/a/5KM3IccD_eU_VxMX_Br_MQ~~/AABEfhA~/js0Em9CwnQHy8kX9IjpbHD2jW8=
w9kmOLV_cJ2MKpbPZRGsRzVJh7-zq-PmyAxwmDklsqBA3cxU9MdsAA1xGeLubmCSQspz-leRM_p=
jFVRECX2JzRVBm3SfJFRhJQVjo60fpfNPUSdHBLKOOc3aBVZZvuQY-TKbUhEZMPD8Igiz5LneQf=
oPRzCz-1wP8Tpc7fkEh4WU0sUslEKizK4ayFjg~~">Programming principles for self-t=
aught front-end developers</a>.</p>=0A<blockquote><p>It can be very temptin=
g to optimise the code right from the start, making sure each line is fast =
and efficient. Often times though, the fast and efficient code is not the m=
ost readable code.</p>=0A<p>When it comes to writing code, we don=E2=80=99t=
 actually spend that much time writing. Instead, we spend time reading the =
code we just wrote, or that already exists and reasoning about it to decide=
 what to write next. So the easier it is to read and reason about the code,=
 the faster we can write the right code.</p>=0A=09</blockquote>=0A<p>Maybe =
that=E2=80=99s a good lesson to learn early on: start by making code very r=
eadable. The move through the classic steps:</p>=0A<blockquote><p>=E2=80=9C=
Make it work, make it right, make it fast=E2=80=9D<p>=0A <cite>Kent Beck, t=
he inventor of Extreme Programming.</cite>=0A    </blockquote>=0D=0A       =
